        'content'    => '<p class="lead">If a <strong>net investment income tax</strong> charge showed up on your return for the first time, you are not alone — this 3.8% surtax catches a lot of people off guard the year their income first crosses the threshold, often from a one-time event like selling a rental property or a large stock gain. This guide explains exactly what counts as investment income, who owes it, how the math actually works, and the handful of income types that are specifically excluded. You can estimate your own capital gains exposure with our <a href="{tool:capital-gains-tax-calculator}">capital gains tax calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>Where this tax came from</h2><p>The Net Investment Income Tax was created by the Health Care and Education Reconciliation Act of 2010, alongside the separate 0.9% Additional Medicare Tax, to help fund the Affordable Care Act. It took effect starting with the 2013 tax year and has applied every year since, with the same rate and the same dollar thresholds the entire time. It is sometimes called the "Medicare surtax" or "Obamacare investment tax" in older articles, which can cause confusion since it has nothing to do with Medicare eligibility or enrollment — it simply uses the health-care law\'s funding mechanism.</p>'
            . '<h2>NIIT at a glance</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Filing status</th><th>MAGI threshold</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Single</td><td>$200,000</td></tr><tr><td>Head of household</td><td>$200,000</td></tr><tr><td>Married filing jointly</td><td>$250,000</td></tr><tr><td>Married filing separately</td><td>$125,000</td></tr><tr><td>Tax rate</td><td>3.8%</td></tr><tr><td>Reporting form</td><td>IRS Form 8960</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These dollar thresholds are set by statute (IRC Section 1411) and, unlike most tax brackets and deduction amounts, they are not adjusted for inflation. They have stayed exactly the same since the tax took effect for the 2013 tax year. That single detail is why NIIT quietly affects more taxpayers every year: as wages and investment values rise with inflation, more people cross a threshold that has not moved at all in over a decade.</p>'
            . '<h2>How the 3.8% tax is actually calculated</h2><p>NIIT is not simply 3.8% of your investment income, and it is not 3.8% of your total income either. It is 3.8% of whichever is smaller:</p><ol><li>Your total net investment income for the year, or</li><li>The amount by which your modified adjusted gross income exceeds your filing status\'s threshold.</li></ol><p>If either of those two numbers is zero or negative, you owe no NIIT at all. This "smaller of" rule matters: someone with a huge amount of investment income but a MAGI only slightly over the threshold pays NIIT on the smaller excess-income figure, not on their full investment income.</p>'
            . '<h2>Worked example</h2><p>A single filer has $220,000 of MAGI, which includes $40,000 of net investment income (dividends, interest and a stock sale gain). Their MAGI exceeds the $200,000 threshold by $20,000. Since $20,000 (the excess over the threshold) is smaller than $40,000 (their actual net investment income), NIIT applies to the $20,000 figure: 3.8% × $20,000 = <strong>$760</strong>.</p><p>Change the numbers slightly — same filer, $300,000 MAGI, same $40,000 of investment income. Now MAGI exceeds the threshold by $100,000, which is larger than the $40,000 of actual investment income, so NIIT applies to the smaller figure: 3.8% × $40,000 = <strong>$1,520</strong>.</p>'
            . '<h2>What counts as net investment income</h2><ul><li>Interest, dividends and capital gains (including the gain on selling a home above the $250,000/$500,000 exclusion)</li><li>Rental and royalty income, unless you qualify as a real estate professional materially participating in the activity</li><li>Income from a passive business you do not actively participate in</li><li>Gains from selling stocks, bonds, mutual funds, and similar investment property</li></ul><p>"Net" means investment expenses — advisory fees, investment interest expense, and certain state income tax allocable to investment income — can be subtracted before the 3.8% applies, which is why keeping good records of investment-related expenses matters for anyone near the threshold.</p>'
            . '<h2>What is specifically excluded</h2><ul><li><strong>Traditional 401(k) and IRA distributions.</strong> Even though this money often originated as investment gains inside the account, the IRS specifically excludes qualified retirement plan distributions from net investment income under Section 1411(c)(5). This is one of the most common points of confusion.</li><li><strong>Wages and self-employment income.</strong> NIIT only applies to investment-type income; ordinary wages are never subject to it (though high earners may separately owe the 0.9% Additional Medicare Tax on wages, a different tax entirely).</li><li><strong>Active business income</strong> from a trade or business you materially participate in.</li><li><strong>Municipal bond interest</strong>, which is already tax-exempt and does not become taxable through NIIT.</li><li><strong>Social Security benefits.</strong></li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Common situations that trigger a surprise NIIT bill</h2><ul><li><strong>Selling a highly appreciated rental property</strong> or a business interest in a single tax year, pushing MAGI well above the threshold even though your regular annual income is normally much lower.</li><li><strong>A large Roth conversion.</strong> While the converted amount itself does not count as net investment income, it does raise your MAGI, which can pull other, genuinely investment-sourced income into NIIT territory for that year. See our <a href="/roth-conversion-before-year-end/">Roth conversion guide</a> for how to weigh this trade-off before converting.</li><li><strong>A big year for dividends or capital gains distributions</strong> from mutual funds, even if you did not personally sell anything.</li><li><strong>Selling a primary home</strong> with a gain above the $250,000 (single) or $500,000 (joint) exclusion — only the taxable portion above the exclusion counts as investment income for NIIT purposes.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Ways to manage NIIT exposure</h2><ul><li><strong>Tax-loss harvesting</strong> reduces net investment income directly by offsetting gains with losses. See our full <a href="/tax-loss-harvesting/">tax loss harvesting guide</a> for the mechanics and the wash-sale rule.</li><li><strong>Timing large sales across multiple years</strong> instead of realizing a huge gain in a single year can keep MAGI under the threshold in any one year.</li><li><strong>Maximizing pre-tax retirement contributions</strong> lowers MAGI directly, which can pull total income back under the threshold.</li><li><strong>Qualified charitable distributions</strong> from an IRA (for those 70½ and older) satisfy required withdrawals without adding to MAGI the way a regular distribution would.</li><li><strong>Municipal bonds</strong> generate income that is excluded from both regular tax and NIIT, which is part of why they are popular with investors already near the threshold.</li></ul><p>None of these moves are free — harvesting losses means selling positions you may have preferred to keep, and shifting into municipal bonds trades a tax exclusion for generally lower yields than taxable alternatives. The right mix depends on your full financial picture, not just the NIIT line on your return in isolation.</p>'
            . '<h2>Common mistakes people make</h2><ul><li><strong>Assuming retirement account withdrawals trigger NIIT.</strong> They generally do not — this is the single most common misunderstanding about this tax.</li><li><strong>Forgetting NIIT when planning a large one-time sale.</strong> A rental property or business sale can push MAGI far above the threshold for that one year alone, even if income is modest every other year.</li><li><strong>Not accounting for investment expenses.</strong> The "net" in net investment income means deductible investment expenses reduce the amount subject to tax.</li><li><strong>Confusing NIIT with the Additional Medicare Tax.</strong> Both use similar-sounding thresholds and both are 2010 health-care-law surtaxes, but NIIT applies to investment income while the Additional Medicare Tax (0.9%) applies to wages and self-employment income — a high earner with both wage and investment income can owe both, calculated separately.</li><li><strong>Waiting until tax season to think about it.</strong> Most of the levers that reduce NIIT — harvesting losses, timing a sale, maxing out a retirement contribution — only work if done before December 31, not after the year has already closed.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>How NIIT stacks with capital gains tax</h2><p>NIIT is an additional surtax layered on top of, not instead of, your regular capital gains tax. A long-term capital gain that falls in the 15% or 20% federal capital gains bracket can also be hit with the 3.8% NIIT if your MAGI is over the threshold, pushing the effective rate on that gain to 18.8% or 23.8% before any state tax. For a high-income investor, this stacking is one of the most overlooked costs of a large stock or property sale in 2026 — it is easy to budget for the capital gains tax rate alone and forget that NIIT can add nearly four more percentage points on top.</p>'
            . '<h2>Does NIIT apply to estates and trusts?</h2><p>Yes, NIIT can also apply to estates and trusts, using a separate, much lower income threshold than the individual thresholds in this guide — the exact 2026 figure is cited inconsistently across secondary sources, so if you are dealing with estate or trust income specifically, confirm the current threshold directly against the IRS Form 8960 instructions rather than relying on a number from this guide, which is written for individual taxpayers.</p>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational and not personal tax advice. Calculating NIIT precisely, especially with rental property, passive activity rules, or a large one-time sale, can get complicated — a CPA can confirm exactly how it applies to your own 2026 return.</p>'
            . '<h2>A near-the-threshold example that changes behavior</h2><p>Consider a married couple filing jointly with $240,000 of wage income and a $30,000 long-term capital gain from selling stock, for a MAGI of $270,000. They are $20,000 over the $250,000 threshold. NIIT applies to the smaller of $30,000 (their net investment income) or $20,000 (the excess over the threshold) — so they owe 3.8% × $20,000 = $760. Now suppose they instead contribute an extra $15,000 to a traditional 401(k) before year-end, lowering MAGI to $255,000. Their excess over the threshold drops to just $5,000, and their NIIT bill falls to 3.8% × $5,000 = $190 — a $570 reduction from one pre-tax contribution decision made before December 31, on top of the separate income-tax savings that contribution already provides.</p>'
            . '<h2>A quick glossary</h2><ul><li><strong>MAGI.</strong> Modified adjusted gross income — your AGI with a small number of items, such as certain foreign income exclusions, added back; for most taxpayers without foreign income, MAGI and AGI are the same number.</li><li><strong>Net investment income.</strong> Investment income such as interest, dividends, capital gains, rental and royalty income, minus directly related investment expenses.</li><li><strong>Form 8960.</strong> The IRS form used to calculate and report NIIT, attached to your Form 1040.</li><li><strong>Additional Medicare Tax.</strong> A separate 0.9% surtax on wages and self-employment income above similar thresholds — easy to confuse with NIIT, but calculated on different income entirely.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Sources</h2><p>IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/individuals/net-investment-income-tax" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Net Investment Income Tax (NIIT) overview</a>; IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-8960" rel="noopener" target="_blank">About Form 8960</a>; IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc559" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Topic 559, net investment income tax</a>. The $200,000/$250,000/$125,000 thresholds are set by IRC Section 1411 and have not been inflation-adjusted since the tax took effect in 2013.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs'       => [
            [ 'What income level triggers the net investment income tax?', 'NIIT applies once your modified adjusted gross income exceeds $200,000 (single or head of household), $250,000 (married filing jointly), or $125,000 (married filing separately). The 3.8% tax then applies to the smaller of your net investment income or the amount your MAGI exceeds that threshold.' ],
            [ 'Does 401(k) withdrawal count toward net investment income tax?', 'No. Qualified retirement plan and IRA distributions are specifically excluded from net investment income under IRC Section 1411(c)(5), even though the money inside the account may have grown from investments. However, a large distribution can still raise your MAGI, which may pull other income into NIIT range.' ],
