    [
        'slug'       => 'home-equity-loan-vs-heloc-vs-line-of-credit',
        'title'      => 'Home Equity Loan vs. HELOC: Which One Fits Your Plan',
        'keyword'    => 'home equity loan vs line of credit',
        'desc'       => 'A home equity loan gives a fixed lump sum; a HELOC gives a flexible, variable-rate credit line. See which one fits your situation.',
        'excerpt'    => 'Same collateral, same risk, very different shape. Here is how to decide between a lump-sum loan and a flexible credit line.',
        'publish_at' => '2026-11-07 21:00',
        'answer'     => 'A home equity loan gives one lump sum at a fixed rate and payment — best for a single, known expense. A HELOC works like a credit card secured by your home, with a variable rate — better for ongoing or uncertain expenses. Both are secured by your home.',
        'takeaways'  => [
            'A home equity loan is a fixed lump sum at a fixed rate; a HELOC is a flexible, variable-rate credit line you draw from as needed.',
            'Rates for each move independently and the "cheaper" option shifts depending on the lender and loan size — always compare live quotes rather than assuming one is always lower.',
            'A HELOC typically has a 5-10 year draw period followed by a repayment period, during which the payment can rise significantly.',
            'Both are secured by your home, and interest on either is only tax-deductible when the money is used to buy, build, or substantially improve the home securing the loan.',
        ],
        'content'    => '<p class="lead">If you are weighing a <strong>home equity loan vs line of credit</strong>, the core difference is simple even though the names sound similar: a home equity loan hands you one lump sum with a fixed rate and fixed payment, while a home equity line of credit (HELOC) gives you an ongoing, variable-rate credit line you draw from over time, similar to a credit card secured by your house. Both let you borrow against the equity you have built up, both use your home as collateral, and both carry the same basic risk — miss payments and you can lose the house. The right choice usually comes down to how certain you are about the total amount you need. Run your own numbers in our <a href="{tool:home-equity-loan-calculator}">home equity loan and HELOC calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>Side-by-side comparison</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Home equity loan</th><th>HELOC</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>How funds are disbursed</td><td>One lump sum at closing</td><td>Draw as needed, up to your limit</td></tr><tr><td>Interest rate</td><td>Fixed for the life of the loan</td><td>Variable, tied to the prime rate</td></tr><tr><td>Monthly payment</td><td>Fixed from day one</td><td>Can change as rates and balance change</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>One known expense with a firm budget</td><td>Ongoing or uncertain expenses, or a standby reserve</td></tr><tr><td>Interest charged on</td><td>The full amount, from day one</td><td>Only the amount you have actually drawn</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Simple installment loan</td><td>Draw period (often 5-10 years), then repayment period</td></tr></tbody></table>'
            . '<h2>Why rates are not a simple "one is always cheaper" answer</h2><p>Current rate surveys genuinely disagree with each other: some show HELOCs pricing noticeably below fixed-rate home equity loans, others show them close together or even slightly higher, and the gap can depend heavily on loan size, loan-to-value, and which lenders a given survey includes. Rather than quote a single number that could be stale within days, the honest answer is: pull live quotes from several lenders for the specific amount and term you need, and compare the full picture — rate, fees, and how the rate can move — not just the starting number. A HELOC\'s rate is variable, so even a lower starting rate can rise over a multi-year draw period in a way a fixed home equity loan rate never will.</p>'
            . '<h2>When a home equity loan makes more sense</h2><ul><li><strong>You know the exact total cost upfront</strong> — a kitchen remodel with a firm contractor quote, consolidating a specific amount of higher-rate debt, or paying a known tuition bill.</li><li><strong>You want payment certainty.</strong> A fixed rate and fixed payment make budgeting simple and protect you from a future rate increase.</li><li><strong>You plan to use all the money close to closing</strong> rather than spreading draws out over years — paying interest on the full amount from day one is wasted cost if you are not using it all right away.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>When a HELOC makes more sense</h2><ul><li><strong>The total cost is uncertain</strong> — a multi-phase home renovation, ongoing medical expenses, or a business that needs flexible access to capital.</li><li><strong>You want a standby safety net</strong> without paying interest until you actually draw funds. Many homeowners open a HELOC and never use most of it, simply to have the option available.</li><li><strong>You are comfortable with a variable rate</strong> and can handle a payment that may rise, particularly once the draw period ends and the loan converts to the repayment period, often with a noticeably higher required payment.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>How much can you borrow with either one?</h2><p>Lenders generally cap combined loan-to-value (CLTV) — all loans secured by the home, including your first mortgage, divided by the home\'s value — at roughly 80% to 85%, with some lenders extending to 90% for well-qualified borrowers. The math is the same for both products: a $400,000 home with a $220,000 remaining mortgage balance and an 85% CLTV cap could support up to $340,000 in combined secured debt, leaving roughly $120,000 available between a new home equity loan or HELOC and the existing mortgage. Our <a href="/mortgage-points-explained/">guide to mortgage points</a> and <a href="/should-i-refinance-my-mortgage/">refinance break-even guide</a> cover related decisions about your first mortgage that can affect how much equity you have available.</p>'
            . '<h2>The draw period and repayment period, explained</h2><p>A HELOC is not a single, static loan — it typically has two distinct phases. During the <strong>draw period</strong> (commonly 5 to 10 years), you can borrow, repay, and borrow again up to your credit limit, often with interest-only minimum payments. Once the draw period ends, the HELOC enters the <strong>repayment period</strong>, during which you can no longer draw new funds and must pay down both principal and interest, which can mean a significantly larger required payment than you were making during the draw period. Some homeowners are caught off guard by this payment jump years into a HELOC — understanding the repayment-period terms before you open the line avoids that surprise.</p>'
            . '<h2>Worked example: a $40,000 kitchen remodel</h2><p>A homeowner needs $40,000 for a kitchen remodel with a signed contractor quote, so the full cost is known upfront.</p><ul><li><strong>Home equity loan at 8.6% fixed, 10-year term:</strong> payment of about $498 a month, with the full $40,000 drawn and accruing interest from day one, even the portion paid to the contractor in a later phase of the project.</li><li><strong>HELOC at a variable rate starting around 7.8%:</strong> during a 10-year draw period, the homeowner draws funds in stages as each phase of the remodel is billed, paying interest only on the amount actually drawn so far — lower total interest paid during construction, but the rate (and payment) can move up or down with the prime rate the entire time, and a larger, fully amortizing payment begins once the draw period ends.</li></ul><p>Because the full cost is already known and fixed, this particular scenario leans toward the home equity loan\'s payment certainty — the example changes if the remodel\'s scope or cost is still uncertain, which argues for a HELOC\'s flexibility instead.</p><p>Notice that the "cheaper" option in this example is not simply whichever has the lower advertised rate — it depends on how predictably the money will be used. A homeowner who draws the HELOC slowly over the project\'s actual timeline, rather than pulling the full $40,000 immediately, pays meaningfully less total interest than either the full-upfront home equity loan or an immediately-maxed-out HELOC, simply because interest only accrues on money actually drawn.</p>'
            . '<h2>What determines your rate on either one</h2><p>Lenders price both products using similar factors: your credit score, combined loan-to-value, debt-to-income ratio, and the loan amount itself. Borrowers with scores above roughly 740 generally see the best-tier pricing on either product, while scores in the 620-679 range typically pay noticeably more, if they qualify at all. A lower CLTV (more equity relative to the amount borrowed) also tends to earn a better rate, since it leaves the lender with more cushion if home values fall. See our guide on <a href="/how-to-improve-credit-score-fast-2026/">improving your credit score quickly</a> if raising your score before applying could meaningfully change the rate you are offered.</p>'
            . '<h2>Closing costs on each</h2><p>Both products typically carry closing costs covering an appraisal, title search, and origination or administrative fees, often totaling roughly 2% to 5% of the loan or credit line amount, though some lenders waive certain fees, particularly on HELOCs, to be more competitive. A HELOC sometimes carries a lower upfront cost than a comparable home equity loan, but may add an annual fee or an early-closure fee if you close the line within the first few years — read the fee schedule closely rather than assuming either product is automatically cheaper to open.</p>'
            . '<h2>Risk is the same for both</h2><p>This is the part people sometimes underweight: both a home equity loan and a HELOC are secured by your home exactly the same way your first mortgage is. Falling behind on either one carries real foreclosure risk, just like missing mortgage payments. Neither product should be used to fund ongoing lifestyle spending you cannot otherwise afford — both are most appropriate for financing that genuinely improves your financial position or your home\'s value, or for consolidating higher-cost debt at a meaningfully lower rate.</p>'
            . '<h2>Is the interest tax-deductible?</h2><p>For either product, interest is deductible only when the borrowed funds are used to buy, build, or substantially improve the home securing the loan, per IRS Publication 936. Using either one to pay off credit cards, cover everyday expenses, or fund something unrelated to the home means the interest is not deductible, regardless of which product you choose — this rule is identical for both.</p>'
            . '<h2>Common mistakes people make</h2><ul><li><strong>Picking based on the lowest advertised rate alone.</strong> A HELOC\'s low introductory or variable rate can rise; compare the realistic total cost, not just the starting number.</li><li><strong>Opening a HELOC for an expense you already know the exact cost of.</strong> If the number is fixed, a home equity loan\'s rate certainty is usually the better fit.</li><li><strong>Forgetting the repayment period is coming.</strong> A HELOC\'s interest-only draw-period payment is not what you will owe forever — budget for the higher repayment-period payment in advance.</li><li><strong>Borrowing more than you need "just in case."</strong> With a home equity loan, you pay interest on the full amount immediately, even the part you do not need yet.</li><li><strong>Assuming the interest is always deductible.</strong> It only is when the funds go toward the home itself, not general spending.</li><li><strong>Not shopping multiple lenders.</strong> Rate, fee and term differences between lenders on the same day can be larger than the structural gap between a home equity loan and a HELOC — get at least three quotes before deciding on either product.</li></ul>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational and not personal financial advice. Compare live rates, fees and terms from multiple lenders for your specific situation before choosing between these two products.</p>'
            . '<h2>Sources</h2><p>IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/publications/p936" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Publication 936, Home Mortgage Interest Deduction</a>; CFPB <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-a-home-equity-line-of-credit-heloc-en-1274/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">what is a home equity line of credit (HELOC)</a>; Bankrate <a href="https://www.bankrate.com/home-equity/current-interest-rates" rel="noopener" target="_blank">current home equity loan and HELOC rates</a>. Rate figures move frequently and vary by source; check a live rate table before applying.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs'       => [
            [ 'What is the difference between a home equity loan and a HELOC?', 'A home equity loan gives you one lump sum upfront at a fixed interest rate and fixed payment. A HELOC gives you a revolving credit line at a variable rate that you draw from as needed, similar to a credit card secured by your home.' ],
            [ 'Is a HELOC always cheaper than a home equity loan?', 'Not necessarily. Current rate surveys disagree, with some showing HELOCs pricing below fixed home equity loans and others showing them close together or higher, depending on loan size and lender. Always compare live quotes rather than assuming one product is always cheaper.' ],
            [ 'How much equity do I need for a home equity loan or HELOC?', 'Most lenders cap combined loan-to-value (CLTV) — your first mortgage plus the new loan, divided by your home\'s value — at 80% to 85%, with some extending to 90% for well-qualified borrowers. The available amount is calculated the same way for both products.' ],
            [ 'What happens when a HELOC\'s draw period ends?', 'The HELOC enters a repayment period, during which you can no longer draw new funds and must pay down both principal and interest. This often means a noticeably higher required payment than the interest-only payments common during the draw period, so it is worth planning for in advance.' ],
            [ 'Is interest on a home equity loan or HELOC tax-deductible?', 'Only if the borrowed money is used to buy, build or substantially improve the home securing the loan, per IRS Publication 936. Using the funds for unrelated expenses, like paying off credit cards or covering everyday costs, makes the interest non-deductible for either product.' ],
            [ 'Can I have both a home equity loan and a HELOC at the same time?', 'Yes, as long as your combined loan-to-value across your first mortgage, the home equity loan, and the HELOC stays within your lender\'s limit, typically 80-85%. Some homeowners use a home equity loan for a known project and keep a separate, smaller HELOC open as a standby reserve.' ],
        ],
    ],
    ];
}
